course: technology
lesson: 6
title: Pencils, paper and school things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Almost everything in your pencil case was made by machines: a pencil is a rod of baked graphite and clay sandwiched between two slats of cedar wood, glued, cut and painted.
explain: The pencil "lead" is not the poisonous metal lead at all, but graphite, a soft grey kind of carbon that leaves tiny flakes on the paper as you write.
explain: Paper starts as trees: wood is chipped and cooked into a mushy pulp, spread on a fast wire mesh to drain, then squeezed and dried between hot rollers into huge rolls.
explain: Look at a magazine picture through a magnifying glass and you will see it is made of tiny dots of just four inks: cyan (a bright sky blue), magenta (a pinky purple), yellow and black.
explain: Cardboard looks weak, but its wavy middle layer makes it strong, like the arches under a bridge.
fact: A pencil is a rod of graphite mixed with clay, baked, laid in grooves between two thin slats of cedar wood, glued, cut and painted.
fact: Pencil "lead" is graphite, a soft form of carbon, not the poisonous metal lead; by a famous estimate one pencil can draw a line about 56 kilometres long.
fact: Paper is made from wood chipped and cooked into pulp, drained on a fast wire mesh, pressed and dried between hot rollers into huge rolls; it can be recycled several times before its fibres get too short.
fact: Wax crayons are made of melted coloured wax poured into moulds, cooled, checked and wrapped in paper labels by machine; the wax melts only when it is hotter than your hand.
fact: A ballpoint pen has a tiny metal ball at its tip, often less than a millimetre wide, that rolls ink from the tube onto the paper; the ink needs to flow down to the ball, so most ballpoints stop working upside down.
fact: Many pen caps have a small hole so air can pass if a child swallows one by accident.
fact: Exercise books are made from big rolls of paper printed with lines, cut, folded, stapled or glued along the spine and trimmed, sometimes all in one machine.
fact: An eraser is rubber or plastic mixed with a gritty powder; rubbing picks up the graphite and the eraser crumbles away with it; before erasers, people rubbed out pencil with bread.
fact: Felt-tip pens have a spongy core soaked in ink that feeds the tip, and they dry out without a cap because the liquid in the ink evaporates, which means it slowly goes off into the air.
fact: Cardboard is made by running paper through hot ridged rollers to make a wavy layer, which is glued between flat sheets, then cut and creased into boxes.
fact: Printed pictures in books and magazines are made of tiny dots of four inks, cyan, magenta, yellow and black, printed one after another by fast rollers.
fact: A glue stick is a soft solid glue in a twist-up tube that dries as its water goes into the air; the first glue stick was invented in 1969.
fact: Rules: never run holding a pencil or pen, and tell an adult at once if a small child swallows a cap or anything else small.
exercise: What is inside a wooden pencil? | A rod of graphite mixed with clay | A stick of the metal lead | A thin tube of black ink | A strip of burnt wood
exercise: Is pencil lead the poisonous metal lead? | No, it is graphite, a soft form of carbon | Yes, so pencils must never be sharpened | Yes, but only in the yellow pencils | No, it is just dried liquid ink pressed into a stick
exercise: What wood are many pencils made of? | Cedar | Cork | Bamboo | Oak bark
exercise: How long a line can one pencil draw, by a famous estimate? | About 56 kilometres | About 56 centimetres | About 5 metres | About 5,600 kilometres
exercise: How is paper made? | Wood is cooked into pulp, drained on a mesh, pressed and dried | Leaves are ironed flat, then stuck together with paste and dried | Cotton cloth is painted white and cut | Plastic is rolled out very thin
exercise: Why can't the same paper be recycled for ever? | Its fibres get shorter each time | It turns into plastic after recycling | Ink can never be washed out of it | The law allows only one recycling
exercise: Why don't wax crayons melt in your hand? | Their wax melts only when it is hotter than your hand | They are frozen at the factory | They are made of coloured stone | Their paper wrapper keeps them cold
exercise: How does a ballpoint pen put ink on paper? | A tiny ball at the tip rolls ink onto the page | A tiny pump inside squirts little drops of ink | The paper sucks the ink out through a small hole | A soft sponge in the tip wipes the ink across the page
exercise: Why does a ballpoint pen stop working upside down? | The ink needs to flow down to the ball | The little ball drops right out of the tip | The ink freezes solid when it is upside down | The paper pushes the ink away when it is above you
exercise: Why do many pen caps have a small hole? | So air can pass if a child swallows one by accident | To let the ink breathe | To save a little plastic | So the cap whistles like a tiny flute when you blow across it
exercise: How does an eraser rub out pencil? | It grips the graphite and crumbles away with it | It melts the graphite with heat | It turns the graphite white | It pushes the graphite deep down inside the paper fibres
exercise: What did people use to rub out pencil before erasers? | Bread | Chalk | Soap | Sand
exercise: Why does a felt-tip dry out with its cap off? | The liquid in its ink evaporates into the air | The felt tip soaks up all the ink and keeps it | Daylight fades the colour away | Air turns the ink into rubber
exercise: Why is cardboard strong? | Its wavy middle layer works like the arches under a bridge | It has thin steel wires hidden inside, running through every sheet | It is made of thin wooden planks | It is soaked all through with glue
exercise: How many inks print every colour in a magazine picture? | Four: cyan, magenta, yellow and black | One: black only | Two: red and blue | Hundreds, one for each colour
exercise: How does a glue stick dry? | Its water goes into the air | It freezes on the paper | It turns into plastic in the light | It needs a hairdryer to set
exercise: What does a machine do to make an exercise book? | Prints lines, cuts, folds, staples or glues, and trims | Weaves the pages from thread | Bakes the pages in an oven | Pours paper into a mould
exercise: A pack has 12 pencils. A class of 30 needs one each. How many packs are needed at least? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 30
test: «don't chew ur pencil, it's lead, u'll be poisoned» What is right? | Pencil lead is graphite, not the metal lead, though chewing pencils is still a bad habit | Yes, every pencil holds poisonous metal | Yes, but only once it is sharpened | No, pencils are made of chocolate
test: «late for the bus, running with me pencil in me hand» What is the rule? | Never run holding a pencil or pen | Hold it point up and just run a bit faster | It is only a problem indoors, not outside | Put it in your mouth instead so your hands are free
test: «pen stopped working, prob cos I was writing on the ceiling lying in bed» What is the reason? | Ink must flow down to the ball, and upside down it can't | Ceilings soak up all the ink | The pen got too cold in bed | The little ball melts when the pen is held upside down for long
test: A felt-tip left without its cap all weekend won't write. Why? | The liquid in the ink evaporated | The felt grew mould over the weekend | Light stole all the colour | Pens wear out from resting
test: «magazines use like a million inks for all them colours» What is right? | Just four inks in tiny dots make the colours | About a million, one for each different shade | Two inks, red and blue, mixed together by hand | Only black ink, which the light makes look coloured
test: What would happen if cardboard had no wavy middle layer? | It would be thin and bend easily under heavy things | It would be twice as strong | It would become see-through | It would become waterproof
test: «paper comes from plastic, innit» What is right? | Paper is made from wood pulp, and often from recycled paper too | Yes, paper is very thin plastic | Paper is made from stone dust mixed with water and dried in the sun | Paper is made from sheep's wool
test: In a cartoon a crayon on a cool windowsill melts into a puddle. What is true? | Crayons soften or melt only when it gets hot, like in a hot car | Crayons melt in any cool place | Crayons melt in your hand in a minute | Crayons never melt at all, not even when they are thrown into a fire
test: A pack has 10 felt-tips. Ben loses 3 and his brother gives him 2. How many does Ben have now? | 9 | 7 | 15 | 5
test: «baby bro swallowed a pen cap, he's breathing fine, don't tell mum» What should you do? | Tell an adult straight away | Keep it secret so nobody gets cross | Give him some water and forget it | Wait a week and see what happens
test: A class of 28 each needs 2 exercise books. How many books is that? | 56 | 30 | 48 | 26
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the core of a pencil. | It consists of graphite combined with clay and contains no metallic lead | It consists of metallic lead and is poisonous | It consists of compressed charcoal from cedar trees | It consists of solidified ink
exam: Identify the principal reason for which corrugated cardboard is able to bear considerable loads. | Its undulating middle layer stiffens it in the manner of a bridge's arches | It is reinforced throughout with fine steel wire concealed within each layer | It is manufactured from solid timber | It is entirely saturated with glue
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the printing of coloured pictures in magazines? | Minute dots of four inks, namely cyan, magenta, yellow and black, are printed in succession | Each colour in the picture is printed with its own separate ink | The picture is painted by hand upon every copy | Black ink alone is employed and coloured by light
exam: Ascertain the minimum number of packets of twelve pencils required in order that each of thirty pupils may receive one pencil. | Three | Two | Four | Thirty
